WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:03.434
-I'm looking for a friend.
-Come on, buddy!

00:00:03.434 --> 00:00:06.067
-I have a friend here today
who was in the live taping.

00:00:06.067 --> 00:00:08.100
-You have a friend here?
-At the taping.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.567
And I didn't -- I think it
was just by coincidence.

00:00:10.567 --> 00:00:12.501
-Oh, really?
-Stand up and wave right now.

00:00:12.501 --> 00:00:15.367
-Here.
-Hey, buddy! I miss you!

00:00:15.367 --> 00:00:16.968
-Hi.
-Yay!

00:00:16.968 --> 00:00:18.801
We did "Dear Evan Hansen"
together.

00:00:18.801 --> 00:00:20.434
-Oh, my God. Not too shabby.

00:00:20.434 --> 00:00:22.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:24.767
Welcome. Welcome to the show.
-That's Mateo Lizcano.

00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:26.934
He was one of
our brilliant Evans

00:00:26.934 --> 00:00:29.300
and he's a stunning performer
and awesome friend.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:31.100
-But here's one of the things
that I love about you,

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:32.467
'cause I know your act
and we all know you from

00:00:32.467 --> 00:00:34.133
"Stranger Things"
and you're great on the show

00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:35.567
and a good actor
and you're funny.

00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:37.667
-Thanks, buddy.
-You can sing, buddy.

00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:39.734
You can real--
-Eh! Thank you.

00:00:39.734 --> 00:00:41.934
-You're a Broadway-trained actor
and you can sing.

00:00:41.934 --> 00:00:43.067
You're phenomenal.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:45.000
-Thank you.
-I mean, it is insane.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.367
-That's very nice.
-It always blows me away.

00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:47.634
I love it.
-Thank you.

00:00:47.634 --> 00:00:48.667
Thank you so much.
-You're very, very talented.

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:49.801
-Thank you.

00:00:49.801 --> 00:00:50.801
-We have to talk about
"Stranger Things."

00:00:50.801 --> 00:00:52.100
-Please!

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.901
-Last time we were on the show,
it was about to --

00:00:53.901 --> 00:00:55.033
second part
was about to come out.

00:00:55.033 --> 00:00:57.033
-That's right.
-Couldn't say anything.

00:00:57.033 --> 00:00:59.534
And then you and
our buddy Joseph Quinn

00:00:59.534 --> 00:01:01.033
just kind of won
the Internet there.

00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:04.400
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:05.968
-I love that guy.

00:01:05.968 --> 00:01:09.000
-Well, what do you like about,
like, when people come up to you

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.300
and talk about
"Stranger Things"?

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.167
Do you just go, "Oh, yeah,
it's cool" or do you --

00:01:12.167 --> 00:01:13.300
-Oh, of course it's cool.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.868
I think it's just --
It's interesting to

00:01:14.868 --> 00:01:16.534
constantly just be
reminded how much

00:01:16.534 --> 00:01:17.868
people have resonated with it.

00:01:17.868 --> 00:01:21.133
I mean, it's something that's
so important to all of us

00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:25.567
and has just been
essential to, like,

00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:28.000
the growth that I've had
over the past --

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.901
over all of my teenage years
into my 20s.

00:01:30.901 --> 00:01:34.501
And it's like --
It's the defining aspect

00:01:34.501 --> 00:01:37.200
of the past 10 years of my life,
practically, the past decade.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.634
-So when people get moved,
you go,

00:01:38.634 --> 00:01:40.367
"Yeah, I'm also moved, too,
because it's part of my life."

00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:41.801
-Exactly, exactly.

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:43.734
It's the fact that at least
something I've been a part of

00:01:43.734 --> 00:01:45.300
can do that for people.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:48.133
It's kind of still sinking in,
I think, and it's wonderful.

00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:49.767
-Yeah, well, this the --
They're wrapping it up.

00:01:49.767 --> 00:01:51.667
-One more, yeah.
-One more.

00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:54.334
Like, they're -- Is it --
It's got to be bittersweet.

00:01:54.334 --> 00:01:55.534
-Of course it is, you know?

00:01:55.534 --> 00:01:56.834
I mean, there's kind of
like a --

00:01:56.834 --> 00:01:59.100
There's an excitement there,
'cause you always want to

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.734
wrap it up and you want to see
how these characters

00:02:01.734 --> 00:02:04.367
are going to finally
develop one last time

00:02:04.367 --> 00:02:07.734
and how they're going to
finish their journeys.

00:02:07.734 --> 00:02:10.334
But, also, it's just --
There's, like, a deep fear

00:02:10.334 --> 00:02:12.701
that's been, like --
Not only has it been amazing,

00:02:12.701 --> 00:02:15.000
but it's been, like,
pretty great job security

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.133
for the past --

00:02:16.133 --> 00:02:17.234
[ Laughter ]

00:02:17.234 --> 00:02:18.567
-Yeah, exactly.

00:02:18.567 --> 00:02:21.567
You're like,
"What am I gonna do?"

00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:22.868
-Back to freelance.

00:02:22.868 --> 00:02:25.634
-Oh, I'm going to bawl
my brains out.

00:02:25.634 --> 00:02:26.934
-Me too.
-I'm going to be so sad.

00:02:26.934 --> 00:02:28.934
But what are your hopes
for Dustin?

00:02:28.934 --> 00:02:30.701
What happens?
-I don't know.

00:02:30.701 --> 00:02:33.300
I think it's in
the best hands it can be.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:36.100
Matt and Ross
know these characters

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.767
better than I think we might.

00:02:38.767 --> 00:02:40.968
And it's really interesting,
'cause they trust us

00:02:40.968 --> 00:02:43.400
all very much
to make our own decisions

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.100
and go about the scenes the way

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:48.334
we've always instinctually
wanted to.

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:51.734
And I've never really
given myself an opportunity

00:02:51.734 --> 00:02:53.033
to think of what
they might want to do,

00:02:53.033 --> 00:02:54.100
and now's
the best time to start.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:55.234
You know what I mean?

00:02:55.234 --> 00:02:57.400
Just really thinking about
what can happen.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.834
I'd just -- I'd love to see
a good launchpad for growth

00:03:01.834 --> 00:03:03.834
and I'd love to see
these characters thrive

00:03:03.834 --> 00:03:07.901
and move on from
the trauma they've endured

00:03:07.901 --> 00:03:09.300
over the past few years.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:11.834
-You think? Yeah, yeah.
A little, yeah.

00:03:11.834 --> 00:03:12.968
But, gosh,
those Duffer brothers,

00:03:12.968 --> 00:03:14.200
yeah, they know what
they're doing.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.868
-Yeah, they're -- they're --
They ain't shabby.

00:03:16.868 --> 00:03:19.434
-Yeah! They're not too shabby.
No, they're not too shabby.

00:03:19.434 --> 00:03:20.734
-That's for sure.

00:03:20.734 --> 00:03:22.200
-Could I talk about
something, too, also?

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.634
We've mentioned
a couple times on our show,

00:03:23.634 --> 00:03:25.634
but you work closely with
an organization

00:03:25.634 --> 00:03:26.767
called CCD Smiles.

00:03:26.767 --> 00:03:29.100
-Yeah!
Thank you so much for the plug.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:32.067
-No, but you do so much
for that organization,

00:03:32.067 --> 00:03:33.534
and I just wanted to say
that's cool that you do that.

00:03:33.534 --> 00:03:34.868
-Thank you.
-I noticed that.

00:03:34.868 --> 00:03:35.968
You know, even when
you're not on our show --

00:03:35.968 --> 00:03:37.968
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Well, thank you.

00:03:37.968 --> 00:03:39.200
Yeah, I love it.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:40.901
-Can you maybe explain
what that is,

00:03:40.901 --> 00:03:42.601
if anyone doesn't know
what it is?

00:03:42.601 --> 00:03:44.033
-Yeah. I think I've talked about
it on the show before,

00:03:44.033 --> 00:03:46.267
-CCD Smiles.

00:03:46.267 --> 00:03:48.801
CCD is short for
cleidocranial dysplasia.

00:03:48.801 --> 00:03:51.467
It's a condition I have myself,
as well as many other

00:03:51.467 --> 00:03:53.267
wonderful people
who I've gotten to meet.

00:03:53.267 --> 00:03:56.734
And my main goal --
Of course, we're there to

00:03:56.734 --> 00:03:59.834
raise money for the people can
get the treatments they need,

00:03:59.834 --> 00:04:02.300
which can range from
dental surgeries

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:05.234
and implants and things
like that and can also --

00:04:05.234 --> 00:04:07.200
There's a whole array of things
that we're just figuring out

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.767
that are associated with CCD.

00:04:08.767 --> 00:04:11.334
That's my biggest goal
is to let this organization

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:13.968
be a beacon for people
and for medical professionals

00:04:13.968 --> 00:04:16.300
so people can learn from
those who are experiencing it.

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:17.634
-Start the conversation.
-Exactly.

00:04:17.634 --> 00:04:19.133
That's what's great is,
you can't expect

00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:21.767
medical professionals and
doctors to know all the answers.

00:04:21.767 --> 00:04:24.033
So it's really quite
a cool feeling

00:04:24.033 --> 00:04:27.167
when you can teach somebody
who's been -- who's an expert

00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:29.300
in so many fields
and help them learn

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:33.067
and help them
treat patients better based on

00:04:33.067 --> 00:04:35.767
the experiences you've had,
and that's what's really cool.

00:04:35.767 --> 00:04:38.501
Like, my family has a bakery,

00:04:38.501 --> 00:04:40.467
and we're doing
this thing where --

00:04:40.467 --> 00:04:46.634
on ccdsmiles.org
and on our bakery's website,

00:04:46.634 --> 00:04:49.634
Joe's Bake Shop --
you can buy, like, cookies,

00:04:49.634 --> 00:04:51.968
and a lot of the proceeds
will go to CCD Smiles.

00:04:51.968 --> 00:04:53.000
-There you go.
So check it out, please.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:56.701
-If you like food --
-We love food.

00:04:56.701 --> 00:04:58.534
I've had them before.
They're delicious.

00:04:58.534 --> 00:04:59.968
-Yes, indeed.
I brought them last time.

00:04:59.968 --> 00:05:01.601
-Yeah, I know. I love it. Yeah.

00:05:01.601 --> 00:05:03.234
Let's talk about "Sweeney Todd."
-Yeah, sure.

00:05:03.234 --> 00:05:04.367
-People are excited
about this, buddy.

00:05:04.367 --> 00:05:06.601
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Me too!

00:05:06.601 --> 00:05:08.267
-This is a big deal.
-It is! It's cool!

00:05:08.267 --> 00:05:11.033
-Back on Broadway,
and you've been doing Broadway

00:05:11.033 --> 00:05:12.067
most of your life.

00:05:12.067 --> 00:05:13.801
-Yeah, since I was 9 years old.

00:05:13.801 --> 00:05:14.968
-9.
-Yeah.

00:05:14.968 --> 00:05:17.934
-I found a photo of you
signing autographs

00:05:17.934 --> 00:05:19.067
backstage at Broadway.

00:05:19.067 --> 00:05:20.701
-This is -- Yeah.
-Look at this little guy.

00:05:20.701 --> 00:05:22.234
-The Palace Theatre.

00:05:22.234 --> 00:05:23.267
Yeah!
[ Audience "Awws" ]

00:05:23.267 --> 00:05:24.501
Oh, boy.

00:05:24.501 --> 00:05:26.801
-What's it like being a kid --
a Broadway kid?

00:05:26.801 --> 00:05:28.968
I mean, just --
I mean, I know there's a lot --

00:05:28.968 --> 00:05:30.133
But you just --

00:05:30.133 --> 00:05:31.801
-Bananas.
-Yeah.

00:05:31.801 --> 00:05:34.000
-It's great. It's so cool.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:35.567
-What are you like,
say, at karaoke?

00:05:35.567 --> 00:05:38.934
Like, what if I -- Are you fun
or are you too good,

00:05:38.934 --> 00:05:40.133
where I just go,
"I got to leave"?

00:05:40.133 --> 00:05:41.767
-No, theater kids are
never fun at karaoke,

00:05:41.767 --> 00:05:43.834
because, like, everyone's there
to, like, be drunk

00:05:43.834 --> 00:05:46.667
and have a good time,
but, like, Broadway kids --

00:05:46.667 --> 00:05:47.801
they're, like, in the bathroom.

00:05:47.801 --> 00:05:49.400
They're like...

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:50.801
They're, like,
getting tea beforehand.

00:05:50.801 --> 00:05:51.934
-You're warming your throat up?

00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:53.434
-They're, like, warming up
and they're like,

00:05:53.434 --> 00:05:55.467
"Ah, I can't mess this up."

00:05:55.467 --> 00:05:56.701
-Yeah, and some of them
are going like...

00:05:56.701 --> 00:05:58.133
♪ I'm a Barbie girl ♪

00:05:58.133 --> 00:05:59.567
♪ In a Barbie world ♪

00:05:59.567 --> 00:06:01.067
And you're like,
"Me, me, me, me, me."

00:06:01.067 --> 00:06:04.300
-But we always, like,
put the biggest damper on

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:07.734
the night by doing, like,
some, like, really sad ballad.

00:06:07.734 --> 00:06:10.534
And they're like,
"Great. Who's this guy?"

00:06:10.534 --> 00:06:12.868
-Like, what would you do?

00:06:12.868 --> 00:06:17.934
-I sang -- There were
a good number of them.

00:06:17.934 --> 00:06:19.534
My first song I ever sang was

00:06:19.534 --> 00:06:21.868
"Fly Me to the Moon"
by Frank Sinatra.

00:06:21.868 --> 00:06:23.701
That was really fun.
-At 9 years old?

00:06:23.701 --> 00:06:25.734
-At 6 years old at this point.

00:06:25.734 --> 00:06:28.434
I remember, I did --
I had a fedora and everything.

00:06:28.434 --> 00:06:30.467
I did this little hat trick
where I did, like, a flip,

00:06:30.467 --> 00:06:31.834
and people just loved it.

00:06:31.834 --> 00:06:33.167
-Yeah. That thing.

00:06:33.167 --> 00:06:37.601
-But I did -- There was one time
I sang "Bring Him Home"

00:06:37.601 --> 00:06:41.000
from "Les Mis," which is,
like, a beautiful song,

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:44.400
but at karaoke night...

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.033
Like, read the room, dude.

00:06:46.033 --> 00:06:47.968
-We want "Don't --" Yeah,
we want "Don't Stop Believin'."

00:06:47.968 --> 00:06:49.033
-Exactly.

00:06:49.033 --> 00:06:50.767
-"Sweeney Todd" --
what do people --

00:06:50.767 --> 00:06:53.868
What should people think
about -- know going into this?

00:06:53.868 --> 00:06:56.501
-I love when people know nothing
about a show going in,

00:06:56.501 --> 00:06:59.300
but what I love about this
production specifically is that

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:00.634
lovers of this show,

00:07:00.634 --> 00:07:03.300
lovers of Stephen Sondheim
will absolutely adore it,

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.133
'cause it's Sweeney
bigger than ever.

00:07:05.133 --> 00:07:06.601
You know what I mean?

00:07:06.601 --> 00:07:11.133
It's back on Broadway, but it's
a huge cast, 26-piece orchestra,

00:07:11.133 --> 00:07:13.100
wonderful group of people
that are just --

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:18.701
It's at this incredible size
that it once was in '79,

00:07:18.701 --> 00:07:20.767
when it opened -- or 1980,

00:07:20.767 --> 00:07:22.467
when it opened originally
on Broadway,

00:07:22.467 --> 00:07:24.300
and we're back to that scale.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:27.567
And to just hear it ring out
in the Lunt-Fontanne,

00:07:27.567 --> 00:07:30.767
which is a beautiful theater,
big house, and --

00:07:30.767 --> 00:07:33.267
-And directed by Thomas Kail.
-Tommy Kail.

00:07:33.267 --> 00:07:36.267
-Who did -- No big deal.
He directed "Hamilton."

00:07:36.267 --> 00:07:38.334
Yeah, no, not a big deal.

00:07:38.334 --> 00:07:40.467
I mean, people are
excited about this.

00:07:40.467 --> 00:07:42.434
-Alex Lacamoire
supervising our music,

00:07:42.434 --> 00:07:44.067
who is just an absolute genius.

00:07:44.067 --> 00:07:45.300
-He is actually a genius.

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:47.400
-Absolute genius,
worked on "Hamilton" with Tommy

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:48.868
and "Dear Evan Hansen,"

00:07:48.868 --> 00:07:50.567
which I got to work on him
with, as well.

00:07:50.567 --> 00:07:51.901
-But if you haven't
seen the play

00:07:51.901 --> 00:07:53.734
or know anything about this,
how do you --

00:07:53.734 --> 00:07:55.801
You describe it as a barber.
-Yeah.

00:07:55.801 --> 00:07:58.167
I think it's quite interesting.

00:07:58.167 --> 00:08:00.434
The pitch
was probably really interesting.

00:08:00.434 --> 00:08:02.234
-Yeah, how would you
even ever pitch this?

00:08:02.234 --> 00:08:05.968
-It's like -- It's a
classic, brilliant, powerful,

00:08:05.968 --> 00:08:08.434
booming musical that
also combines, like,

00:08:08.434 --> 00:08:10.767
a slasher comedy.

00:08:10.767 --> 00:08:14.467
And that's pretty wild
to describe,

00:08:14.467 --> 00:08:17.234
but it's just
quintessential Sondheim.

00:08:17.234 --> 00:08:20.200
When you talk about
his best works,

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:23.400
this is always talked about
as one of the top three,

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:26.067
if not, like, the show.

00:08:26.067 --> 00:08:28.534
-A barber who murders everyone.
-A lot of people.

00:08:28.534 --> 00:08:30.767
-If you sit down in his
barber chair, he kills you.

00:08:30.767 --> 00:08:31.934
-And it's not even,
like, a spoiler.

00:08:31.934 --> 00:08:34.234
It's called "The Demon Barber of
Fleet Street."

00:08:34.234 --> 00:08:37.434
Like, he's probably
not the coolest.

00:08:37.434 --> 00:08:40.000
-But he -- Then he works with
a pie shop...

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:41.167
-Yes, indeed.

00:08:41.167 --> 00:08:44.133
-...to kind of help
dispose of the bodies.

00:08:44.133 --> 00:08:45.234
-It's fun.

00:08:45.234 --> 00:08:47.000
-Yeah, it's fun
for the whole family.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:49.033
-It's great! They sing songs.
-Everyone check it out.

00:08:49.033 --> 00:08:51.133
[ Cheers and applause ]
It's going to be so much fun.

00:08:51.133 --> 00:08:53.400
Opening this weekend.
We love you, buddy.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:56.534
Break a leg. Gaten Matarazzo.

00:08:56.534 --> 00:08:58.934
"Sweeney Todd"
is now playing on Broadway

00:08:58.934 --> 00:09:01.234
at the Lunt-Fontanne Theatre.

00:09:01.234 --> 00:09:02.334
More "Tonight Show"
after the break.

00:09:02.334 --> 00:09:03.467
Stick around.

